===Etymology 1===
{{ja-kanjitab|むこ|yomi=k|alt=壻,聟}}
&#42;{{IPAfont|/moko/}} → <span style="display:inline-block; vertical-align: middle;><span style="float:left;">*{{IPAfont|⟨muko<sub>1</sub>⟩}} → *{{IPAchar|/mukʷo/}}</span><span style="float:left;clear:both;">*{{IPAfont|⟨mo<sub>(1)</sub>ko<sub>1</sub>⟩}} → *{{IPAchar|/mʷokʷo/}}</span></span><ref>Frellesvig, B. & Whitman, J. (2008) "[http://conf.ling.cornell.edu/whitman/VowelsofProto-Japanese.Martfest.pdf The Vowels of Proto-Japanese]", ''Proto-Japanese: Issues and Prospects''. Amsterdam: John Benjamins Pub. Co.</ref> → {{IPAchar|/muko/}}

Found in the ''{{w|Nihon Shoki}}'' of 720 {{CE}} with the [[ideographic]] spelling {{m|ja|聟}}.<ref name="KDJ">{{R:Kokugo Dai Jiten}}</ref>

Although the reading ''muko'' is not confirmed in {{m+|ojp|-|w=1}} documents, the presence of cognate words suggests that this may be from {{inh|ja|jpx-pro|*moko|sort=むこ}} (cognate with {{cog|ryu|婿|tr=mūku}}, {{cog|xug|婿|tr=mufu}}, {{cog|mvi|婿|tr=muku}} and the ''moko'' below). This would be the a result of a phonological change, whereby non-final *{{IPAchar|/o/}} in Proto-Japonic nouns shifted to become {{IPAfont|/u/}} in Central Old Japanese.

In regard to the derivation, there are some theories proposed, however many of them are associated with the verbs {{m|ja|向かう|tr=mukau||to [[go]] [[towards]]}} and {{m|ja|迎える|tr=mukaeru||to [[receive]]}}, both derived from {{m|ja|向く|tr=muku||to [[turn]] [[toward]]}}, from {{cog|jpx-pro|*muk-}}, from the idea of "the facing party, the other person (of a pair)"; see also {{m|ja|向こう|tr=mukō||the other side, the facing side}}. Theories to explain this inconsistency in the proto-forms have not been published yet.

